# =============================================================================
# CommitAI web app — environment
# Copy to `apps/web/.env` (gitignored) and fill in. Never commit real secrets.
# =============================================================================

# PostgreSQL connection string used by Prisma (datasource `db` in
# prisma/schema.prisma).
#
# Offline Prisma commands work with any syntactically valid URL:
#   pnpm --filter web exec prisma validate
#   pnpm --filter web exec prisma generate
#   pnpm --filter web exec prisma migrate diff \
#     --from-empty --to-schema-datamodel prisma/schema.prisma --script
#
# APPLYING migrations (`prisma migrate deploy`) and the DB-gated integration
# tests need a REACHABLE Postgres: a local install, a managed instance, or a
# container. When DATABASE_URL is unset/unreachable those tests skip cleanly
# (see LIMITATIONS.md step 3); the unit tests never need a DB.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/commitai?schema=public"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI provider selection (build steps 4–6).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which concrete `AIProvider` the app runs on: "gemini" (default) or "groq". Both
# implement the same interface behind `lib/ai/provider.ts`, so switching is a config
# change only — no code above `lib/ai/` knows which vendor is answering. Unset means
# "gemini", so existing deployments are unaffected. An unrecognised value THROWS
# rather than falling back silently (CLAUDE.md rule 1); only the SELECTED provider's
# API key is required.
#
# The evidence-privacy guarantee below is provider-INDEPENDENT: it is enforced in
# `lib/ai/` by 13 always-on tests, not by any vendor's terms.
AI_PROVIDER="gemini"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gemini (Google) — the default AI provider (build steps 4–6).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API key for the model. Get a free-tier key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# The provider makes NO network call without this; when it is unset the AI unit
# tests use an in-test double and the live end-to-end test skips cleanly (see
# LIMITATIONS.md §8). This is the MODEL api key only — never a wallet/deployer key
# (CLAUDE.md rule 3).
#
# PRIVACY CAVEAT: on Google's FREE tier, prompts and responses may be used to
# improve Google's products. Staying on the free tier is a DELIBERATE, documented
# tradeoff (LIMITATIONS.md §10.1, item 13) — acceptable only because raw evidence
# never reaches the model: uploaded bytes, `Evidence.contentText` and blob storage
# keys are unreachable from `lib/ai/`, enforced by 13 always-on tests in
# `lib/ai/privacyBoundary.test.ts` — which police EVERY provider's egress, not only
# Gemini's. The model sees goal/verification metadata, hashes, and the user's own
# chat text. A paid tier (data not used for training) or self-hosted inference behind
# the same `AIProvider` interface remains the recommended production upgrade; it is
# not required for the evidence guarantee.
GEMINI_API_KEY=""

# Model id. Defaults to the current free-tier Flash model when unset. Override to
# pin a version or use the auto-updating `gemini-flash-latest` alias. The default
# is confirmed at build time per build-prompt §1.
GEMINI_MODEL="gemini-3.7-flash"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Groq — the alternative AI provider (only when AI_PROVIDER="groq").
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API key for the model. Get one at https://console.groq.com/keys
# Used only when AI_PROVIDER="groq"; the provider makes NO network call without it,
# and `groqFromEnv()` throws rather than attempting a keyless request. When it is
# unset the Groq unit tests still run in full (they inject a transport double) and
# only the live end-to-end test skips. This is the MODEL api key only — never a
# wallet/deployer key (CLAUDE.md rule 3).
#
# Groq speaks an OpenAI-compatible API, so `lib/ai/groq.ts` needs no vendor SDK —
# it is a plain `fetch` to api.groq.com, and it is the ONLY file in the app allowed
# to name that host (asserted by the egress guard in privacyBoundary.test.ts).
#
# PRIVACY CAVEAT: check Groq's own data-use terms for the tier you are on — free and
# promotional tiers at any vendor may use prompts/responses for product improvement.
# The evidence guarantee does NOT depend on that answer: raw evidence is unreachable
# from `lib/ai/` whichever provider is selected, so the model sees goal/verification
# metadata, hashes, and the user's own chat text either way. A tier that excludes
# data from training remains the recommended production upgrade for the chat text.
GROQ_API_KEY=""

# Model id. Defaults to Groq's current PRODUCTION-tier tool-use model when unset.
# Override to pin a version or to trade quality for latency/cost (e.g. the smaller
# `openai/gpt-oss-20b`). Must be a function-calling capable model — the 19-tool
# agentic loop in `lib/ai/runner.ts` depends on it. Prefer a Production-tier id: Groq
# says Preview models "should not be used in production" and may be pulled with little
# warning.
#
# Groq RETIRES model ids on a published schedule:
#   https://console.groq.com/docs/deprecations
# `llama-3.3-70b-versatile` was this default until 2026-08-21 and was SHUT DOWN on
# 2026-08-16, so it now returns an error; the default moved to Groq's own recommended
# replacement. If a call ever fails with a decommissioned-model message, check that
# page and set a current id here — no code change is needed. Note gpt-oss models do
# not do PARALLEL tool calls; the agentic loop just takes more rounds, same result.
GROQ_MODEL="openai/gpt-oss-120b"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Evidence storage (build step 7) — the off-chain blob store behind
# `EvidenceStorage`. Raw evidence (photos, files, text) lives OFF-chain only;
# ONLY its sha256 content hash is ever eligible to be anchored on-chain (§9/§10).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Driver selector. "local" (default) writes blobs to disk; "s3" uses any
# S3-compatible bucket (AWS S3, Supabase Storage, Cloudflare R2, MinIO) via real
# SigV4-signed HTTP. Switching is a config change only — both drivers implement the
# same interface and produce the same content-addressed key (see LIMITATIONS.md §13).
EVIDENCE_STORAGE_DRIVER="local"

# Root directory for the local-disk driver. Defaults to `apps/web/.evidence-store`
# (gitignored) when unset. Content-addressed + wallet-namespaced underneath.
EVIDENCE_STORAGE_DIR=""

# S3 driver (only when EVIDENCE_STORAGE_DRIVER="s3"). Bucket + region are required;
# ENDPOINT is optional for AWS (derived as https://s3.<region>.amazonaws.com) and
# required for Supabase/R2/MinIO. A custom endpoint defaults to path-style addressing.
EVIDENCE_S3_BUCKET=""
EVIDENCE_S3_REGION=""
EVIDENCE_S3_ENDPOINT=""
# EVIDENCE_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE="true"   # override auto-detection if needed
# EVIDENCE_S3_PREFIX=""                  # optional key prefix inside the bucket
# Credentials — read separately, never logged. This key moves blobs only, never funds.
EVIDENCE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
EVIDENCE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Evidence content hardening (LIMITATIONS.md §13, item 10).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Content sniffing (declared type held against the real magic bytes) and image
# EXIF/GPS/XMP scrubbing are ALWAYS on — no configuration, no opt-out.
#
# Malware scanning is the one part that needs an engine, so it is opt-in and OFF by
# default: uploads are then stored unscanned and the hardening report says so
# (`scanned: false`) rather than claiming a scan that never ran. Set to "clamd" to
# scan every upload through a real ClamAV daemon over its INSTREAM protocol
# (`docker run -p 3310:3310 clamav/clamav`). FAIL-CLOSED: once a driver is selected,
# an unreachable or erroring scanner refuses the upload with 503 instead of letting
# it through. Selecting "clamd" without EVIDENCE_CLAMD_HOST throws at startup.
EVIDENCE_MALWARE_SCAN="off"
EVIDENCE_CLAMD_HOST=""
# EVIDENCE_CLAMD_PORT="3310"           # default 3310
# EVIDENCE_CLAMD_TIMEOUT_MS="10000"    # whole-scan deadline, default 10000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On-chain: BOT Chain testnet + CommitmentVault (build step 8).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RPC / chain id / explorer for BOT Chain testnet. These have live-verified
# DEFAULTS (chain id 968, `eth_chainId -> 0x3c8`), so read-only chain calls work
# as soon as the RPC is reachable — you only need to set them to override.
BOTCHAIN_TESTNET_RPC_URL="https://rpc.bohr.life"
BOTCHAIN_TESTNET_CHAIN_ID="968"
BOTCHAIN_TESTNET_EXPLORER_URL="https://scan.bohr.life"

# Deployed CommitmentVault address. NO default (CLAUDE.md rule 1): until a real
# deploy is recorded this stays empty, chain writes/reads report "not configured"
# rather than a fabricated address, and the chain-aware tools decline honestly.
# Set it after `forge script ... --broadcast` (see README "On-chain deployment").
# A value that is set-but-malformed fails loudly rather than degrading silently.
COMMITMENT_VAULT_ADDRESS=""

# Block the vault was deployed in — the default start of a historical event replay
# (POST /api/chain/reconcile, LIMITATIONS.md item 12). Optional and NOT a secret:
# unset simply means a full replay starts at block 0, which is correct but slower;
# callers can always pass an explicit `fromBlock`. A wrong value only makes a replay
# shorter or slower, never wrong — but a set-but-malformed value THROWS rather than
# silently skipping real history, which is the bug the reconciler exists to fix.
COMMITMENT_VAULT_DEPLOYMENT_BLOCK=""

# Backend attestor key — server-only, NO default. MONEY-SAFETY (CLAUDE.md rules
# 2–3): this key can ONLY attest. The contract lets the attestor call exactly
# registerMilestone / requestCompletion / approveCompletion / setAttestor — NONE
# of which move value (every transfer is depositor-signed and pull-based). There
# is no code path, on this key or anywhere in the backend, that can move a
# depositor's funds; `contractClient.getAttestorClient()` exposes only those four
# methods and is frozen (asserted in contractClient.safety.test.ts). It is read
# ONLY by the server and never rides inside a logged config object. Leave empty to
# run reads/prepare-only tools without attesting. This is NEVER a deployer/wallet key.
#
# It also cannot approve a completion BY ITSELF: approveCompletion now takes an
# EIP-712 receipt signed by the separate AI-verifier key below (two-of-two).
ATTESTOR_PRIVATE_KEY=""

# AI verifier key — server-only, NO default, SIGNING ONLY (LIMITATIONS.md item 11).
# It signs the VerificationReceipt that binds approveCompletion to one specific AI
# decision: {commitmentId, goalId, milestoneRef, confidence, evidenceHash,
# verificationHash, modelVersionHash, deadline}. The contract recovers the signer and
# reverts InvalidVerificationReceipt unless it is exactly the on-chain `aiVerifier`,
# so the attestor cannot invent a confidence value and the AI cannot approve without
# the attestor. MONEY-SAFETY: `getReceiptSigner()` returns a frozen object with only
# { address, signReceipt } — no wallet client, no transport, no writeContract, no
# sendTransaction (asserted in lib/chain/receipt.safety.test.ts), so this key cannot
# send a transaction at all, let alone move funds. Its address MUST equal the vault's
# INITIAL_AI_VERIFIER / aiVerifier(), and MUST differ from the attestor's address
# (a contract invariant). Leave empty to run without approving. NEVER a wallet key.
AI_VERIFIER_PRIVATE_KEY=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# App origin + SIWE session (build step 9 — wallet auth).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Canonical origin of the running app. Used for (a) the same-origin / CSRF check
# on state-changing API requests and (b) the expected SIWE domain (anti-phishing).
# Same-origin requests always pass; set this to your real https origin in
# production (behind a proxy it pins the check to the external host).
APP_ORIGIN="http://localhost:3000"
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# SECRET — iron-session cookie encryption key (min 32 chars). NO default
# (CLAUDE.md rule 1): the server refuses to open a session without it rather than
# fall back to a weak key. Generate one and paste it into apps/web/.env only:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
SESSION_PASSWORD=""
